From: Tamer Ahmed <tamerahmed@microsoft.com>
Date: Mon, 15 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] zebra: retire a sync MAC's neighbours when the MAC goes local-inactive

A sync MAC, one still advertised by the ES peers, can go locally inactive
by two different routes, and the neighbours hanging off it are retired by
neither.

zebra_evpn_add_update_local_mac() is the path an ageing MAC actually
arrives on. The dataplane reports the MAC as an update carrying
local_inactive rather than as a delete, and the function sets
ZEBRA_MAC_LOCAL_INACTIVE and informs BGP about the MAC alone.

zebra_evpn_del_local_mac() is the other route. For a sync MAC it marks the
entry locally inactive, re-installs it in the dataplane and returns early,
so zebra_evpn_process_neigh_on_local_mac_del() is never reached.

Either way the neighbours keep their locally active state, and nothing
else retires them: zebra keeps re-installing a sync neighbour as
REACHABLE, so rt_netlink never derives local_inactive from the NUD state.

That matters because zebra_evpn_neigh_send_add_del_to_client() only sets
ZEBRA_MACIP_TYPE_PROXY_ADVERT for a neighbour that is locally inactive.
The MAC-IP route stays non-proxy, bgpd derives the MAC-only proxy state
from the MAC-IP paths so that stays non-proxy too, and each peer keeps
seeing the segment as active on peer. A non-proxy advertisement cancels
the peer's hold timer, which is the only thing that would retire the MAC,
so the entry is never withdrawn even once the host has gone.

Reproduced on a two-leaf setup by letting the host go silent with every
link left up. Both leaves aged the MAC to local-inactive as expected, but
the neighbours stayed active, the proxy bit was never set, and the MAC was
still present after 15 minutes.

Retire the neighbours on both transitions, using the same sequence the
neighbour delete path already applies to a sync entry: mark locally
inactive, recompute readiness for BGP, tell the client, and leave the
entry in the dataplane where the peers still own it.

Signed-off-by: Tamer Ahmed <tamerahmed@microsoft.com>
---
diff --git a/zebra/zebra_evpn_mac.c b/zebra/zebra_evpn_mac.c
index cb7c13e197..8fd72a2b1e 100644
--- a/zebra/zebra_evpn_mac.c
+++ b/zebra/zebra_evpn_mac.c
@@ -2352,6 +2352,13 @@ int zebra_evpn_add_update_local_mac(struct zebra_vrf *zvrf,
 		zebra_evpn_mac_send_add_del_to_client(mac, old_bgp_ready,
 						      new_bgp_ready);
 
+	/* A MAC that has just aged out locally takes its neighbours with it.
+	 * This is the path an ageing MAC actually arrives on: the dataplane
+	 * reports it as an update with local_inactive set, not as a delete.
+	 */
+	if (local_inactive && !old_local_inactive)
+		zebra_evpn_process_neigh_on_local_mac_inactive(zevpn, mac);
+
 	/* Process all neighbors associated with this MAC, if required. */
 	if (upd_neigh)
 		zebra_evpn_process_neigh_on_local_mac_change(zevpn, mac, 0,
@@ -2396,6 +2403,13 @@ int zebra_evpn_del_local_mac(struct zebra_evpn *zevpn, struct zebra_mac *mac,
 				zebra_evpn_mac_is_ready_for_bgp(mac->flags);
 			zebra_evpn_mac_send_add_del_to_client(mac, old_bgp_ready,
 							      new_bgp_ready);
+
+			/* the neighbours behind this MAC are no longer locally
+			 * reachable either, and unless they follow, the MAC-IP
+			 * route stays non-proxy and the ES peers never start
+			 * the hold timer that retires the MAC
+			 */
+			zebra_evpn_process_neigh_on_local_mac_inactive(zevpn, mac);
 		}
 
 		/* In EXT-LEARN mode as well, the MAC will be reprogrammed as static,
diff --git a/zebra/zebra_evpn_neigh.c b/zebra/zebra_evpn_neigh.c
index 290789e179..782201258c 100644
--- a/zebra/zebra_evpn_neigh.c
+++ b/zebra/zebra_evpn_neigh.c
@@ -1053,6 +1053,50 @@ void zebra_evpn_process_neigh_on_local_mac_del(struct zebra_evpn *zevpn,
 	}
 }
 
+/*
+ * Process all neighbors associated with a sync MAC that has just gone locally
+ * inactive.
+ *
+ * Distinct from the MAC-delete case above: the MAC is still advertised by the
+ * ES peers, so these neighbours must not be withdrawn. They are marked locally
+ * inactive and re-advertised, which turns the MAC-IP route into a proxy route.
+ * Without that the neighbour stays locally active, bgpd keeps reporting the
+ * segment as active-on-peer, and the peers cancel the hold timer that would
+ * otherwise retire the MAC once the host is gone from every leaf.
+ */
+void zebra_evpn_process_neigh_on_local_mac_inactive(struct zebra_evpn *zevpn,
+						    struct zebra_mac *zmac)
+{
+	struct zebra_neigh *n = NULL;
+	struct listnode *node = NULL;
+	bool old_bgp_ready;
+	bool new_bgp_ready;
+
+	for (ALL_LIST_ELEMENTS_RO(zmac->neigh_list, node, n)) {
+		if (!CHECK_FLAG(n->flags, ZEBRA_NEIGH_LOCAL))
+			continue;
+
+		if (CHECK_FLAG(n->flags, ZEBRA_NEIGH_LOCAL_INACTIVE))
+			continue;
+
+		old_bgp_ready = zebra_evpn_neigh_is_ready_for_bgp(n);
+		SET_FLAG(n->flags, ZEBRA_NEIGH_LOCAL_INACTIVE);
+		new_bgp_ready = zebra_evpn_neigh_is_ready_for_bgp(n);
+
+		if (IS_ZEBRA_DEBUG_EVPN_MH_NEIGH)
+			zlog_debug("sync-neigh vni %u ip %pIA mac %pEA local-inactive on mac del f 0x%x",
+				   zevpn->vni, &n->ip, &n->emac, n->flags);
+
+		zebra_evpn_neigh_send_add_del_to_client(n, old_bgp_ready,
+							new_bgp_ready);
+
+		/* the entry stays in the dataplane, owned by the peers */
+		zebra_evpn_sync_neigh_dp_install(n, false /* set_inactive */,
+						 false /* force_clear_static */,
+						 __func__);
+	}
+}
+
 /*
  * Process all neighbors associated with a MAC upon the MAC being remotely
  * learnt.
diff --git a/zebra/zebra_evpn_neigh.h b/zebra/zebra_evpn_neigh.h
index b7f3d1556d..370718e655 100644
--- a/zebra/zebra_evpn_neigh.h
+++ b/zebra/zebra_evpn_neigh.h
@@ -231,6 +231,8 @@ void zebra_evpn_process_neigh_on_remote_mac_add(struct zebra_evpn *zevpn,
 						struct zebra_mac *zmac);
 void zebra_evpn_process_neigh_on_local_mac_del(struct zebra_evpn *zevpn,
 					       struct zebra_mac *zmac);
+void zebra_evpn_process_neigh_on_local_mac_inactive(struct zebra_evpn *zevpn,
+						    struct zebra_mac *zmac);
 void zebra_evpn_process_neigh_on_local_mac_change(struct zebra_evpn *zevpn,
 						  struct zebra_mac *zmac,
 						  bool seq_change,
